According to https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/616d6d4e75df54841ad2a27f68b7d88c207f4203/pkg/volume/util/operationexecutor/node_expander.go#L99-L106, the kubelet will expand the RWX volume. The operation will fail because the csi driver does not support node volume expansion.


code snippet from a given driver:

```
&csi.ControllerExpandVolumeResponse{
		...
		NodeExpansionRequired: false,
	}, nil
```

The NodeExpansionRequired indicates that kubelet should not expand the volume on the node. There is a workaround in the `legacyCallNodeExpandOnPlugin` function like this:

```go
...
	_, resizeErr := expandableVolumePlugin.NodeExpand(rsOpts)
	if resizeErr != nil {
		// This is a workaround for now, until RecoverFromVolumeExpansionFailure feature goes GA.
		// If RecoverFromVolumeExpansionFailure feature is enabled, we will not ever hit this state, because
		// we will wait for VolumeExpansionPendingOnNode before trying to expand volume in kubelet.
		if volumetypes.IsOperationNotSupportedError(resizeErr) {
			klog.V(4).InfoS(volumeToMount.GenerateMsgDetailed("MountVolume.NodeExpandVolume failed", "NodeExpandVolume not supported"), "pod", klog.KObj(volumeToMount.Pod))
			return true, nil
		}
...
```

Since the RecoverFromVolumeExpansionFailure feature-gate is enabled by default, the workaround is not triggered for the RWX volume. 

In order to fix this issue, we need to copy the above workaround to the [`expandOnPlugin` function](https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/master/pkg/volume/util/operationexecutor/node_expander.go#L146).

